begin;

select public.seed_cipmn_mock_module('CIPMN-MOD-007','[["Project scope versus product scope","Stakeholders discuss only the features of the final system and omit the project work needed for training, migration, transition and acceptance.","Define both product scope and project scope so the required outcome and all work needed to deliver it are clear.","Document only product features because project-management work is not part of scope.","Treat training and transition as unplanned operational activities.","Use the budget as the sole definition of scope.","Product scope describes the result''s features and functions; project scope describes the work required to create and transition that result."],["Scope management planning","No one has agreed how scope will be defined, validated, controlled or changed, and different teams use inconsistent documents.","Prepare a scope management plan defining processes, roles, baselines, validation and change-control arrangements.","Allow each contractor to manage scope according to its own preference.","Wait for the first dispute before defining scope controls.","Use only the project schedule as the scope management plan.","A scope management plan establishes how scope will be developed, approved, validated and controlled."],["Requirements gathering","The project team relies on a single senior manager''s assumptions and has not consulted users, regulators, operations or affected communities.","Use appropriate elicitation techniques with the full range of relevant stakeholders and document sources and decisions.","Proceed because seniority makes the manager''s assumptions complete.","Consult users only after the deliverable is built.","Ask the contractor to define requirements without client participation.","Complete scope depends on systematic requirements elicitation from affected, knowledgeable and authoritative stakeholders."],["Scope statement","The approved document lists a project title and budget but provides no deliverables, acceptance criteria, exclusions, assumptions or constraints.","Develop a detailed scope statement that defines deliverables, boundaries, acceptance criteria, assumptions and constraints.","Use the title as the complete scope statement.","Leave exclusions undocumented so the project can remain flexible.","Replace acceptance criteria with a general promise of quality.","A clear scope statement establishes what is included, what is excluded and how deliverables will be accepted."],["Project boundaries and exclusions","A customer assumes long-term maintenance is included, while the project team believes responsibility ends at commissioning.","Clarify and approve the project boundary, exclusions, transition responsibilities and post-project support arrangements.","Avoid discussing the issue so it does not expand the scope.","Assume the customer''s expectation is included because it was mentioned informally.","Let the operations team discover responsibility after handover.","Explicit boundaries and exclusions prevent conflicting expectations and support controlled transition."],["Work Breakdown Structure and scope baseline","The team has an approved scope statement but no decomposition into manageable deliverables and work packages.","Create and approve a WBS and WBS dictionary as part of the scope baseline.","Use only a list of staff assignments.","Convert every meeting action into a top-level deliverable.","Begin procurement without defining work packages.","The scope baseline normally includes the scope statement, WBS and WBS dictionary and provides the basis for planning and control."],["Validate scope and formal acceptance","A deliverable is technically complete, but the authorised customer has not inspected it or signed acceptance.","Perform scope validation against agreed acceptance criteria and obtain formal acceptance from the authorised stakeholder.","Declare acceptance because the project team believes the work is complete.","Use payment of a supplier invoice as the only acceptance evidence.","Wait until all project deliverables are complete before discussing any acceptance.","Validate Scope formalises customer or sponsor acceptance of completed deliverables based on agreed criteria."],["Monitoring scope performance","The team reports schedule and cost but does not compare completed work with the approved scope baseline.","Monitor deliverable status, requirements coverage and scope variance against the baseline.","Assume scope is controlled whenever expenditure is within budget.","Review scope only during project closure.","Measure progress by the number of meetings held.","Scope control requires evidence that authorised work is being completed and unauthorised work is not being introduced."],["Preventing scope creep","Team members add ''small'' stakeholder requests during execution without recording cumulative effects on effort, cost or schedule.","Stop informal additions and route all proposed changes through the agreed change-control process.","Allow small changes because individually they appear inexpensive.","Remove the requests from reports but continue implementing them.","Ask the team to work overtime so the baseline remains unchanged.","Scope creep is uncontrolled expansion of scope; even small changes require visibility and authorised assessment."],["Change request documentation","A sponsor verbally asks for a major redesign, but there is no documented rationale, requested outcome, urgency or decision record.","Document a formal change request with sufficient information for analysis, decision and traceability.","Implement immediately because the sponsor has authority.","Reject the request because verbal requests are never valid.","Record only the new deadline and omit the design change.","A documented change request creates a traceable basis for impact analysis, authorisation and implementation."],["Integrated change impact analysis","A proposed scope addition is evaluated only for direct cost and not for schedule, resources, quality, risk, procurement or benefits.","Perform an integrated impact analysis across all affected baselines, constraints, risks and intended value.","Approve the change if the direct cost fits within contingency.","Assess schedule impact only after the change has been implemented.","Ignore downstream effects because the request concerns scope.","Integrated change control considers the full system of project impacts rather than one constraint in isolation."],["Change Control Board and decision authority","A technical lead approves a change that exceeds delegated tolerance and commits the organisation to substantial additional cost.","Refer the change to the authorised decision body or Change Control Board in accordance with governance thresholds.","Accept the technical lead''s decision because technical expertise overrides financial authority.","Allow every workstream lead to approve unlimited changes.","Defer all changes to project closure.","Change decisions must be made by the authority defined in governance arrangements and delegated tolerances."],["Updating baselines after approval","A change is approved and implemented, but the scope statement, WBS, schedule, budget, requirements and communications remain unchanged.","Update all affected baselines, plans, traceability records and stakeholder communications in a controlled manner.","Keep original records unchanged so the initial plan is preserved.","Update only the meeting minutes.","Allow each team to maintain its own revised version.","Approved changes must be integrated into controlled project records so execution and reporting use the authorised current baseline."],["Managing organisational resistance to change","Users resist a new system because roles will change, training is unclear and they believe decisions were made without their input.","Diagnose the sources of resistance, engage affected people, communicate the case for change and provide participation, support and training.","Label all resistance as misconduct and proceed without engagement.","Send one general email immediately before go-live.","Hide the role changes until the system is launched.","Resistance is best managed by understanding concerns and using credible communication, participation, capability building and leadership support."],["PESTLE and change context","A major transformation plan ignores a new regulation, inflation, technology limitations, social expectations and environmental requirements.","Use a contextual tool such as PESTLE to assess external change drivers and adapt the change and project plan.","Focus only on internal team preferences.","Assume external factors are outside management responsibility.","Review the environment only after implementation.","PESTLE helps identify political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental factors affecting change and project viability."]]'::jsonb);

commit;
